1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,460
Hi everyone, and welcome to the Digital Factory Practicum at WenQuest.

2
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:14,806
This is Lecture 6, maintenance and reliability. Should we stop for service?

3
00:00:16,326 --> 00:00:18,426
Here's what happened.

4
00:00:18,826 --> 00:00:26,243
Every gear shaft for the robot plant's extra order of joint reducers goes

5
00:00:26,263 --> 00:00:28,586
through grinder GRD-01,

6
00:00:28,606 --> 00:00:30,929
the plant's bottleneck.

7
00:00:31,329 --> 00:00:37,362
On Monday morning the factory hub flagged it as overdue:

8
00:00:37,382 --> 00:00:42,226
the interval is 500 hours and it has run 620.

9
00:00:42,626 --> 00:00:45,659
A failure stops it for 8 hours,

10
00:00:45,679 --> 00:00:52,651
costs 1,200 dollars to repair and loses 200 dollars of margin per hour;

11
00:00:52,671 --> 00:00:59,446
a service stops it for 3 hours and costs 215 in labour and materials.

12
00:00:59,846 --> 00:01:03,672
The supervisor says the rush order comes first;

13
00:01:03,692 --> 00:01:07,026
the maintenance planner says stop it now.

14
00:01:07,426 --> 00:01:07,896
Who's right?

15
00:01:09,416 --> 00:01:11,313
Why machines fail.

16
00:01:12,833 --> 00:01:19,774
First, the failure rate: given that a machine has run t hours without failing,

17
00:01:19,794 --> 00:01:23,254
the chance per hour that it fails next.

18
00:01:23,654 --> 00:01:27,350
Statistics show three phases, like a bathtub.

19
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:31,993
New machines suffer infant mortality and the rate falls;

20
00:01:32,013 --> 00:01:37,702
the cure is run-in and acceptance, and frequent dismantling makes it worse.

21
00:01:38,102 --> 00:01:45,746
In the middle the rate is roughly constant and failures come from outside;

22
00:01:45,766 --> 00:01:47,922
service doesn't help.

23
00:01:48,322 --> 00:01:55,029
Then comes wear-out: bearings, guides and seals wear and the rate rises.

24
00:01:55,429 --> 00:01:59,009
That's the phase preventive maintenance fights.

25
00:02:00,529 --> 00:02:07,239
To describe these phases engineers mostly use the Weibull distribution.

26
00:02:07,639 --> 00:02:10,597
F of t is the chance of failing before t;

27
00:02:10,617 --> 00:02:15,899
differentiate and divide by the reliability and you get the failure rate.

28
00:02:16,299 --> 00:02:22,332
On screen beta goes from 0.7 up to 4; watch the red failure rate:

29
00:02:22,352 --> 00:02:27,639
below one it falls, at one it's flat, above one it rises.

30
00:02:28,039 --> 00:02:33,553
Mechanical wear is usually between two and four.

31
00:02:33,953 --> 00:02:39,770
Eta is the characteristic life: whatever beta is,

32
00:02:39,790 --> 00:02:45,368
the failure probability at eta is 63.2 percent.

33
00:02:46,888 --> 00:02:51,351
The grinder has beta three and eta 900 hours,

34
00:02:51,371 --> 00:02:56,332
the parameters the digital factory uses to age it.

35
00:02:56,732 --> 00:03:05,403
At the 500-hour interval the failure probability is 15.8 percent; now,

36
00:03:05,423 --> 00:03:08,383
at 620 hours, it's 27.9;

37
00:03:08,403 --> 00:03:17,323
and the failure rate has more than quadrupled between 300 and 620 hours.

38
00:03:17,723 --> 00:03:25,223
Mean life is 804 hours, and by 800 hours the probability is past half.

39
00:03:26,743 --> 00:03:29,157
Probability, then money.

40
00:03:30,677 --> 00:03:37,097
A decision doesn't need F of 620; we know it hasn't failed.

41
00:03:37,497 --> 00:03:44,757
We need the chance that a machine which has run t hours fails within H more.

42
00:03:45,157 --> 00:03:53,377
That's a conditional probability: one minus R of t plus H over R of t.

43
00:03:53,777 --> 00:04:05,827
For the grinder: 700 over 900 cubed minus 620 over 900 cubed is 0.1436,

44
00:04:05,847 --> 00:04:09,397
so P is 13.4 percent.

45
00:04:09,797 --> 00:04:13,690
Service it now and the clock goes back to zero:

46
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:16,605
0.07 percent for the next 80 hours,

47
00:04:16,625 --> 00:04:21,601
about one hundred and ninetieth of the risk without service.

48
00:04:23,121 --> 00:04:25,621
This is Virtual lab 6-1.

49
00:04:26,021 --> 00:04:33,496
The orange band is the next 80 hours, and t slides from 100 hours onward.

50
00:04:33,896 --> 00:04:37,281
The band keeps its width,

51
00:04:37,301 --> 00:04:45,453
but the curve gets steeper and the probability inside grows:

52
00:04:45,473 --> 00:04:52,400
3.8 percent at 300 hours, 13.4 at 620, 20.7 at 800.

53
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:57,414
That's what beta above one means: the longer you wait,

54
00:04:57,434 --> 00:04:59,902
the riskier the next stretch.

55
00:05:01,422 --> 00:05:03,402
One special case to remember.

56
00:05:03,802 --> 00:05:11,086
When beta is one, t cancels in the exponent and P depends only on H.

57
00:05:11,486 --> 00:05:19,663
A machine that has run 620 hours and one just serviced have exactly the same

58
00:05:19,683 --> 00:05:23,869
chance of failing in the next 80 hours:

59
00:05:23,889 --> 00:05:27,429
9.5 percent each with eta of 800.

60
00:05:27,828 --> 00:05:30,506
That's memorylessness.

61
00:05:30,906 --> 00:05:37,519
Random failures can't be fought with scheduled service; you need protection,

62
00:05:37,539 --> 00:05:40,486
condition monitoring or a standby.

63
00:05:42,006 --> 00:05:44,226
Now turn probability into money.

64
00:05:44,626 --> 00:05:48,018
One failure of the grinder:

65
00:05:48,038 --> 00:05:56,739
a 1,200 repair plus 8 hours stopped at 200 an hour on the bottleneck,

66
00:05:56,759 --> 00:05:59,393
2,800 dollars in all.

67
00:05:59,793 --> 00:06:11,164
The expected loss without service is 13.4 percent of 2,800, about 375 dollars.

68
00:06:11,564 --> 00:06:16,875
Mind you, that's an expectation, not money you'll certainly spend:

69
00:06:16,895 --> 00:06:22,287
most of the time nothing happens, sometimes you lose 2,800 at once.

70
00:06:22,687 --> 00:06:27,130
If one failure would make you miss a key customer's delivery,

71
00:06:27,150 --> 00:06:28,667
add that penalty too.

72
00:06:30,187 --> 00:06:33,667
The factory hub offers three options.

73
00:06:34,067 --> 00:06:37,636
A, do nothing: 375 dollars expected,

74
00:06:37,656 --> 00:06:44,017
and a failure stops it for 8 hours and may delay the rush order.

75
00:06:44,417 --> 00:06:46,648
B, service now:

76
00:06:46,668 --> 00:06:58,354
215 plus 600 of margin lost while the bottleneck stops for 3 hours, about 817.

77
00:06:58,754 --> 00:07:02,040
C, service after the shift:

78
00:07:02,060 --> 00:07:09,879
it runs 4 more hours with only a 0.63 percent chance of failing,

79
00:07:09,899 --> 00:07:11,961
about 235 in all.

80
00:07:12,361 --> 00:07:19,621
C is cheapest: risk nearly zero, and no production time lost on the bottleneck.

81
00:07:20,021 --> 00:07:24,041
The supervisor and the planner were each half right.

82
00:07:25,561 --> 00:07:28,622
Choosing the interval.

83
00:07:30,142 --> 00:07:33,122
One step further: what should the rule be?

84
00:07:33,522 --> 00:07:38,739
A common policy is age-based maintenance: service at T hours,

85
00:07:38,759 --> 00:07:43,460
and if it fails first, repair it and restart the clock.

86
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:51,134
In a cycle, with probability R of T it reaches T and you pay for a service;

87
00:07:51,154 --> 00:07:56,872
with probability F of T it fails and you pay for a failure.

88
00:07:57,272 --> 00:07:59,132
That's the numerator.

89
00:07:59,532 --> 00:08:07,977
The mean cycle length, the integral of R from zero to T, is the denominator.

90
00:08:08,377 --> 00:08:14,037
Divide and you get the long-run cost per operating hour.

91
00:08:14,437 --> 00:08:19,882
As T goes to infinity it's run to failure:

92
00:08:19,902 --> 00:08:24,697
3.48 dollars an hour for the grinder.

93
00:08:26,217 --> 00:08:31,157
This is Virtual lab 6-2 with T sweeping back and forth.

94
00:08:31,557 --> 00:08:37,006
First, servicing after the shift at 215 a time:

95
00:08:37,026 --> 00:08:42,708
the optimum is 312 hours at 1.04 dollars an hour,

96
00:08:42,728 --> 00:08:50,970
seventy percent below run to failure; the current 500 hours costs 1.30,

97
00:08:50,990 --> 00:08:52,017
too long.

98
00:08:52,417 --> 00:08:58,041
Now switch to stopping production, 600 more of lost margin each time:

99
00:08:58,061 --> 00:09:02,703
service is dearer and the optimum moves out to 535 hours,

100
00:09:02,723 --> 00:09:05,157
so 500 is practically optimal.

101
00:09:05,557 --> 00:09:08,605
The cheaper the service, the more often;

102
00:09:08,625 --> 00:09:14,511
and moving it out of production time saves far more than tuning the interval.

103
00:09:16,031 --> 00:09:23,874
On screen two identical machines run 20,000 hours on the same random lifetimes:

104
00:09:23,894 --> 00:09:29,049
blue is serviced every T hours, red runs to failure.

105
00:09:29,449 --> 00:09:35,929
At first the lines are close, and red may even be lower when it's lucky;

106
00:09:35,949 --> 00:09:39,631
the longer they run, the clearer the gap,

107
00:09:39,651 --> 00:09:45,138
and their slopes approach C of T and the run-to-failure 3.48.

108
00:09:45,538 --> 00:09:53,358
Judge a policy by its long-run average, not by one or two lucky runs.

109
00:09:54,878 --> 00:09:56,596
Checking and doing.

110
00:09:58,116 --> 00:10:00,576
Where do beta and eta come from?

111
00:10:00,976 --> 00:10:02,117
From failure records.

112
00:10:02,517 --> 00:10:06,378
The simplest measure is MTBF:

113
00:10:06,398 --> 00:10:13,337
30 days at 16 hours with 3 failures gives 160 hours.

114
00:10:13,737 --> 00:10:18,791
It's only an average and can't show whether the rate is rising;

115
00:10:18,811 --> 00:10:20,241
with more records,

116
00:10:20,261 --> 00:10:26,282
fit a Weibull to the hours since last service at each failure and recompute

117
00:10:26,302 --> 00:10:27,329
the interval.

118
00:10:27,729 --> 00:10:35,358
And keep probability apart from one outcome: with a 13 percent risk,

119
00:10:35,378 --> 00:10:40,645
one or two of ten students' grinders will fail.

120
00:10:41,045 --> 00:10:46,545
Not failing doesn't prove skipping the service was right.

121
00:10:48,065 --> 00:10:51,665
Finally, do it yourself in the digital factory.

122
00:10:52,065 --> 00:10:58,481
Each of its 13 machines has its own Weibull parameters, the model we've used,

123
00:10:58,501 --> 00:11:02,326
and they really fail with those probabilities.

124
00:11:02,726 --> 00:11:06,735
In Lab 18 you read the asset register,

125
00:11:06,755 --> 00:11:14,368
calculate the probability and expected loss, and the platform checks it.

126
00:11:14,768 --> 00:11:19,060
In the command centre you compare the three options,

127
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:21,963
choose one and submit your reasons;

128
00:11:21,983 --> 00:11:26,193
the hub's recommendation stays hidden until you do.

129
00:11:26,593 --> 00:11:32,063
Then you raise the service order, the instructor approves it,

130
00:11:32,083 --> 00:11:36,653
and you go through lockout, service and acceptance,

131
00:11:36,673 --> 00:11:39,893
leaving a service record in ERPNext.

132
00:11:40,293 --> 00:11:43,236
Finally the shop keeps running:

133
00:11:43,256 --> 00:11:49,833
count failures on serviced and unserviced machines and estimate MTBF.

134
00:11:51,353 --> 00:11:59,304
Every joint of a collaborative robot has a precision reducer whose grease

135
00:11:59,324 --> 00:12:03,563
ages and whose bearings and teeth wear:

136
00:12:03,583 --> 00:12:06,293
a classic beta above one.

137
00:12:06,693 --> 00:12:14,058
Take beta 2.5 and eta 20,000 hours, illustrative values.

138
00:12:14,458 --> 00:12:23,733
A joint that has run 14,000 hours has a 15.0 percent chance of failing in the

139
00:12:23,753 --> 00:12:25,061
next 2,000;

140
00:12:25,081 --> 00:12:28,924
a freshly greased one, only 0.3.

141
00:12:29,324 --> 00:12:34,984
That's why makers specify regreasing by hours.

142
00:12:35,384 --> 00:12:40,530
Better still, the controller can read each joint motor's current;

143
00:12:40,550 --> 00:12:44,504
friction creeping up means the reducer is wearing,

144
00:12:44,524 --> 00:12:47,524
and servicing on condition is cheaper.

145
00:12:47,924 --> 00:12:56,243
The WQR-105 reducers our plant makes go into exactly these joints.

146
00:12:57,763 --> 00:13:02,540
An everyday example: changing a car's oil.

147
00:13:02,940 --> 00:13:10,479
The manual says every 5,000 kilometres; stretching it to 8,000 is usually fine,

148
00:13:10,499 --> 00:13:16,412
but one engine failure costs as much as dozens of oil changes.

149
00:13:16,812 --> 00:13:22,802
When to change it is probability times loss against a small, certain cost:

150
00:13:22,822 --> 00:13:25,725
the same problem as today's grinder.

151
00:13:27,245 --> 00:13:28,444
Back to the start.

152
00:13:28,844 --> 00:13:35,482
The grinder has a 13.4 percent chance of failing in the next 80 hours,

153
00:13:35,502 --> 00:13:40,047
an expected loss of 375 dollars without service.

154
00:13:40,447 --> 00:13:46,759
Of the three options, servicing after the shift is cheapest,

155
00:13:46,779 --> 00:13:48,764
about 235 expected:

156
00:13:48,784 --> 00:13:56,363
the rush order is untouched and the risk over the next 80 hours drops to

157
00:13:56,383 --> 00:13:58,263
about 0.7 percent.

158
00:13:58,663 --> 00:14:03,090
And if services are always done after the shift,

159
00:14:03,110 --> 00:14:09,483
the optimal interval is about 312 hours; the current 500 is too long.

160
00:14:09,883 --> 00:14:13,726
Changing it needs the plant manager's approval:

161
00:14:13,746 --> 00:14:20,383
submit it under the asset's details and the ERPNext maintenance schedule follows.

162
00:14:21,903 --> 00:14:27,765
Now Virtual lab 6-1. On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

163
00:14:27,785 --> 00:14:34,243
The numbers in the results panel are blurred here; reading them is your job.

164
00:14:34,643 --> 00:14:42,240
First choose GRD-01 and read the chance of failing in the next 80 hours after 620,

165
00:14:42,260 --> 00:14:49,114
and the expected loss. Check them against the hand calculation from class.

166
00:14:49,514 --> 00:14:57,147
Then drag the hours run from zero to twelve hundred and watch the probability

167
00:14:57,167 --> 00:14:59,036
in the orange band.

168
00:14:59,056 --> 00:15:04,999
Press Service now: how far does it drop, and by what factor?

169
00:15:05,399 --> 00:15:09,978
Next press the beta-equals-one check and drag again.

170
00:15:09,998 --> 00:15:16,433
Does the probability still change? Why is scheduled service useless then?

171
00:15:16,833 --> 00:15:21,477
Finally press Play and watch the saw-tooth of the clock:

172
00:15:21,497 --> 00:15:26,558
service at the interval resets it, and now and then it fails.

173
00:15:26,578 --> 00:15:32,139
Try intervals of 300 and 800 hours and count services and failures.

174
00:15:33,659 --> 00:15:37,831
Virtual lab 6-2: choosing the service interval.

175
00:15:37,851 --> 00:15:44,163
The curve is the long-run cost per operating hour of age-based service;

176
00:15:44,183 --> 00:15:47,552
the red dashed line is run to failure.

177
00:15:47,952 --> 00:15:50,080
Press service after shift,

178
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:56,028
drag the interval to the lowest point and read the optimum and its cost.

179
00:15:56,048 --> 00:16:01,812
Then press stop during production and see which way the minimum moves.

180
00:16:02,212 --> 00:16:06,160
Finally play the race between two identical machines,

181
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:09,903
one serviced every T hours and one run to failure,

182
00:16:09,923 --> 00:16:15,219
on the same random lifetimes for 20,000 hours, and compare their costs.

183
00:16:15,239 --> 00:16:19,187
I won't give the answers here; they're yours to find.

184
00:16:19,587 --> 00:16:24,447
Both labs come with a guide and a report template on the platform.

185
00:16:25,967 --> 00:16:31,922
After class you'll do Lab 18 in the WenQuest digital factory.

186
00:16:31,942 --> 00:16:39,072
All 13 machines on the floor age by today's Weibull model and really fail

187
00:16:39,092 --> 00:16:41,521
with these probabilities.

188
00:16:41,921 --> 00:16:47,137
First calculate the overdue machine's probability and expected loss,

189
00:16:47,157 --> 00:16:49,216
and the platform checks it.

190
00:16:49,236 --> 00:16:53,297
Then compare the three options in the command centre,

191
00:16:53,317 --> 00:16:56,839
choose one and give reasons. Until you submit,

192
00:16:56,859 --> 00:17:00,381
the factory hub's recommendation stays hidden.

193
00:17:00,781 --> 00:17:05,574
Then raise the service order, the instructor approves it,

194
00:17:05,594 --> 00:17:09,881
and you go through lockout, service and acceptance.

195
00:17:09,901 --> 00:17:12,752
Finally let the shop keep running,

196
00:17:12,772 --> 00:17:19,001
count failures on serviced and unserviced machines, and estimate the MTBF.

197
00:17:20,521 --> 00:17:24,257
The assignment has three tasks. One, calculation:

198
00:17:24,277 --> 00:17:27,246
if the grinder is left until 800 hours,

199
00:17:27,266 --> 00:17:31,922
what are the probability and expected loss? Then a CNC lathe:

200
00:17:31,942 --> 00:17:37,901
does servicing it now pay, and why is the answer different from the grinder's?

201
00:17:38,301 --> 00:17:39,455
Two, design:

202
00:17:39,475 --> 00:17:46,400
use C of T to find the lathe's optimal interval and compare it with the

203
00:17:46,420 --> 00:17:48,161
current 600 hours.

204
00:17:48,561 --> 00:17:50,890
Three, the Lab 18 report,

205
00:17:50,910 --> 00:17:57,845
focusing on how you compared the options and how your choice compares with

206
00:17:57,865 --> 00:17:59,443
the hub's advice.

207
00:18:00,963 --> 00:18:04,466
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

208
00:18:04,486 --> 00:18:10,211
for a machine with beta below one, should you shorten the service interval?

209
00:18:10,611 --> 00:18:14,874
A grinder 300 hours after its last service:

210
00:18:14,894 --> 00:18:19,855
what's its chance of failing in the next 80 hours?

211
00:18:20,255 --> 00:18:22,898
You can take the quiz three times,

212
00:18:22,918 --> 00:18:26,815
and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

213
00:18:28,335 --> 00:18:30,475
Three things to remember.

214
00:18:30,875 --> 00:18:34,035
One: service only helps against wear;

215
00:18:34,055 --> 00:18:38,417
scheduled service pays only when beta is above one,

216
00:18:38,437 --> 00:18:42,800
and with beta of one failure doesn't depend on age.

217
00:18:43,200 --> 00:18:46,747
Two: decide with the conditional probability,

218
00:18:46,767 --> 00:18:52,138
the chance that a machine which has run t hours fails in the next H,

219
00:18:52,158 --> 00:18:56,340
times the loss per failure; that's the expected loss,

220
00:18:56,360 --> 00:19:00,224
to be weighed with the service cost and delivery.

221
00:19:00,624 --> 00:19:06,962
Three: set the interval with C of T; the cheaper the service, the more often,

222
00:19:06,982 --> 00:19:13,484
and moving it out of production time often saves more than tuning the interval.

223
00:19:13,884 --> 00:19:21,781
After class: Assignment 6, Quiz 6 and the Lab 18 report.

224
00:19:22,181 --> 00:19:25,947
The virtual labs, the digital-factory lab,

225
00:19:25,967 --> 00:19:32,257
the assignment and the quiz are all on the WenQuest learning platform,

226
00:19:32,277 --> 00:19:38,296
at learn.wenquestrobotics.com. Sign up for free and start learning.

227
00:19:38,696 --> 00:19:45,477
Next time it's people and shifts, and what to do when a key technician is away.

228
00:19:45,497 --> 00:19:46,596
See you then.
